A||N||11||Formula88||17" Wheel source||||5||
Z||000000||Formula88||09-07-2001||12:24 PM||||OK, I'm looking for some wheels for my Formula.  17x9 45mm offset rear and 17x8 (45mm offset?) front.  I can find lots of 17" wheels, but usually only 7 or 7.5" wide.  Any suggestions on places to check?  I'd like to avoid having to get modular wheels at $500 a pop.  That's just not in the budget.  Even if it was, I don't want to pay that much for wheels.  I'd like to keep it under $200 each, if I can.<P>The style I'm looking for is a black, gray, or anthracite 5 spoke similar in appearance to the 96 Corvette Gran Sport wheels.  I'm not looking for a copy, but a similar style.<P>Thanks!<P>------------------<BR>"God made us brothers.  Prozac made us friends."||||reg||5
Z||000001||1FST2M6||09-07-2001||12:48 PM||||good luck.. the Konig Monsoon is available in 17x7-7.5-8 and 9 but the offest won't work for our cars.. the wider wheel is set for a mustang/camaro. a TSW Hokenheim R is 17x8 and will work fine for about $180 ea. most everything else in that width will be a modular unit.. the ROH Reflex is a 1 piece that kinda cheap if you can still find em. <A HREF="http://www.racinghart.com" TARGET=_blank>www.racinghart.com</A>  for a 3 piece, <A HREF="http://www.fikse.com" TARGET=_blank>www.fikse.com</A>  and HRE (don't know their site) wheels arent' cheap. espesially wideer then most +1-3" stock preplacement ones.  ask Doug Greenup at <A HREF="http://www.wheel1.com" TARGET=_blank>www.wheel1.com</A>  ||||reg||1
Z||000002||Formula88||09-07-2001||01:29 PM||||Thanks for the info!  Yeah, I'd like to keep the price down if I can, but if I gotta spend the bucks, I'd rather do that than not get what I want.  I'm just trying to find the best bargain I can.  I want to run 255/40-17s out back, so a 9" rim is mandatory.  Up front, I'll probably go with either 215/45-17 or 225/45-17, so I could use a 7.5" rim, but would like to go with the 8" to keep my options open.  Both are very close to the stock tire diameter. <P>Also, since this is an 88, do I need to use a different offset on the front?  West Coast Fiero says I can use a 45mm offset all the way around, but I don't want the front to stick out of the wheel wells.||||reg||1
Z||000003||1FST2M6||09-07-2001||01:41 PM||||a 7-7.5" can use a 40 or 42mm offset up front the rear with an 8" will need a 45 mm offset. I'm sure you've seen Chris' red car.. it's got 45 in all 4 corners running DOT approved road racing tires Yokohama A032s. it doesn't look that bad at all.  what engine mods have you done to need a 255? thats an awful LOT of tire. you need poly bushings and better tie rods with that much grip... the stock units are pretty flimsy. my old car under nitrous split the bushings. and bent a tie rod... my current car has 3/8" threaded rod with heim ends. ||||reg||1
Z||000004||Formula88||09-07-2001||02:07 PM||||Well, it's all stock for now, so the tires are mainly for looks, and better handling.  However, mods are planned in the future.  Probably either a 3800SC or N*, unless budget dictates something less.  Then I'll likely go with a 3.4 with NOS.  Poly's not a bad idea.  I'm planning on running KYB GR-2 in the back, and either the KYB Gas-A-Just or GR-2 up front.  Also considering the Fiero Store's sport lowering springs, unless I find a better alternative other than coil overs.  <P>This car will be a regular driver, so I'm not setting it up for racing, but I want superior looks and handling (not necessarily in that order) while mainting at least some degree if driveability.  Things like a 4 puck clutch are right out.  Probably go with the kush-lok next time around, or one of the Carbon-Kevlar discs offered by West Coast Fiero.||||reg||6
Z||000005||artherd||09-07-2001||03:36 PM||||Hrm, I belive 17x8" tyres will fit 255series tyres, (infact Slammed Fiero I belive runs that exact setup with Pzero Pirellis)<P>I would not recomend anything larger(read: wider) than 17x7 with 215/45 upfront, you don't need it in this car, and after this point, you see deminishing returns (the car gets *harder* to turn, and *more* likely to plow under power due to gravel,etc.)<P>If you are up for it, there is an excellent writeup on adapting the 88 Fiero to use Porsche Boxster wheels (and their 5x113mm bolt spacing) turns out they're perfect for our cars 17 or 18x7 front, 9" rear. Offsets are just right.  You need to do (have done) some machine work for the 113mm pattern, and rear coilovers though  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> <P>Bright side is you can pick up a set of regular boxster wheels off ebay real cheap, usually with tyres, when one of their well heeled owners buys a $10k set of 19 or 20" heh.<BR><P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/88ttop.jpg">  <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top Metalic Red<BR>88 Formula, Silver<BR>87 Coupe, Metalic Red<BR>"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>                -Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||3
Z||000006||Formula88||09-07-2001||03:53 PM||||Well, according to Tire Racks specs on the tires I'm looking at, a 255/40-17 will fit on a rim from 8.5"-10" in width and the Measured Rim Width is 9", so I'd prefer to go with the mfg. recommended width of 9", but 8 won't work - at least not according to the tire manufacturer.  If I go with an 8 or 8.5" rim, I'll either go with 235's or 245's, whichever size I can get closest to the original tire diameter (probably 235/45-17).  I can use a 7" or 7.5" wide wheel up front if I'm running no larger than 225/45-17, so that may be what I go with.  An 8" rim will fit both of those sizes, and allow me to go as wide as a 235 or 245/40 on the front (although I probably wouldn't, I want to keep my options open).  A 215/45 on an 8" wide wheel should handle and steer as well as the same tire on a 7" wide wheel.  Unless I'm missing something here.<P>And no, I'm not interested in Boxter wheels.  One of the things I mentioned was not going to coil overs.  So I definately don't want to do coil overs AND have to do machine work too.<P>Thanks for the input, though.  I'm looking for as much info as I can get.||||reg||6
Z||000007||AusFiero||09-07-2001||05:39 PM||||I can supply a good range of 17X7 and up  wheels for between $190 and $350 a rim delivered to your door. I will check with my supplier on the larger sizes if your interested. Can even do 18"s if you really want to put some big rubber under there.<P>------------------<BR>    <IMG SRC="http://www.jadeweb.com.au/fiero/ausfiero1.gif">   <BR><A HREF="http://www.jadeweb.com.au/fiero" TARGET=_blank>My Fiero</A> | <A HREF="http://www.fieroshop.com.au" TARGET=_blank>Fiero Accessories</A>||202.139.20.3||reg||5
Z||000008||Rick 88||09-07-2001||06:20 PM||||Formula,<P>I have 17x7.5 in wheels on my 88 GT. They are .40 offset. My front tires are 215/45/17 and the rears are 245/45/17. The fronts don't stick in compairson to the rears due to the difference in tire width. My speedometer is only off by about 1 mph. I also have 88 coupe front springs with 1 coil removed to lower the front. My ride is very good since the coupe springs are softer rate to begin with. When viewed down the side the tires fill the fenders equally. I don't have any rubbing or interference at full lock. LMK if you need any additional info.<P>Rick<BR>yellow 88 GT 5-speed||||reg||7
Z||000009||Sleeper||09-07-2001||07:54 PM||||don't you need coil-overs to run any wheel bigger than an 8in?||||reg||1
Z||000010||qwikgta||09-07-2001||08:29 PM||||I think 88 are diff, the spring/strut looks diff on my 88 and 87.  The spring is shaped diff and looks smaller in diameter at the base, "the part that would interfere w/ tire", maybe this is why you can run a 9" rim in the rear.  I will be going to coil-over soon so it will not matter much.  <P>Rob <P>------------------<BR>qwikgta@yahoo.com<BR>88 T-Top coupe, 65K, Header no Cat, 16" GTZ rims, short shifter, swaybar, open element air cleaner, hood vents.<BR>http://www.homestead.com/robsfieroproject   <BR>http://www.vafieros.com<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sigpic2.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000011||Formula88||09-08-2001||06:53 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by AusFiero:<BR><B>I can supply a good range of 17X7 and up  wheels for between $190 and $350 a rim delivered to your door. I will check with my supplier on the larger sizes if your interested. Can even do 18"s if you really want to put some big rubber under there.<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>That would be cool.  I'm looking for 17" in a style similar to the black 5 spoke rims on the 96 Gran Sport Corvette.  Doesn't have to be a replica, but a similar style.  Black, anthracite, charcoal, but with a silver rim.  And I want the lug nuts visible.  I don't like rims with covers over the lugs.  <P>I'm thinking 17x9 or 17x8 rear and 17x8 or mabye 17x7.5 front.  A 45mm offset on the rear wheels and a 40-42mm offset on the fronts if I go with 17x7.5, and a 45mm offset if I go with the 17x8.  Clear as mud, huh?||||reg||1
